Description
📝 Description
EscrowState.platform and EscrowState.maintainer are set once in initialize and read from storage everywhere (auth::require_platform, auth::require_maintainer). storage::set_admin is only ever called from inside initialize. There is no method anywhere in the contract that changes any of these three addresses afterward.

This is called out as a known limitation in docs/arch.md ("A single platform wallet is trusted for all releases in this contract instance"), but there is currently no mitigation — if the platform or maintainer key is lost or compromised, the escrow is stuck with no recovery path short of redeploying and manually migrating funds.

✅ Requirements
Add transfer_admin(env, new_admin), update_platform(env, new_platform), and update_maintainer(env, new_maintainer) entry points
All three require the current stored admin's auth (not the current platform or maintainer key, so a compromised key can be rotated out)
All three require an active escrow (auth::require_active)
Emit AdminTransferred, PlatformUpdated, MaintainerUpdated events respectively
🎯 Acceptance Criteria
 transfer_admin changes the stored Admin address and emits AdminTransferred
 update_platform changes EscrowState.platform and emits PlatformUpdated
 update_maintainer changes EscrowState.maintainer and emits MaintainerUpdated
 Only the current stored admin can call any of the three methods
 After rotation, require_platform/require_maintainer immediately reflect the new addresses and old keys are rejected
 Non-admin callers are rejected for all three methods
📁 Expected files to change/structure
trustless-oss/src/lib.rs — add transfer_admin, update_platform, update_maintainer
trustless-oss/src/events.rs — add AdminTransferred, PlatformUpdated, MaintainerUpdated event keys and emit helpers
trustless-oss/src/test.rs — rotation happy-path and rejected-caller tests
